[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100888,
      "id": "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a",
      "title": "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "77cbec7a-0f7d-5e08-afb5-b96c4a1360f4",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=- 4 y\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=2 e^{- 4 x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=- 10 e^{- 4 x}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=2 e^{- 4 x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(2 e^{- 4 x}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=2\\cdot (-4)\\,e^{- 4 x} \\\\ &=- 8 e^{- 4 x} \\\\ &\\not\\equiv - 10 e^{- 4 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=2 e^{- 4 x}$ and $y\\prime=- 8 e^{- 4 x}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}- 4 y \\\\ - 8 e^{- 4 x} &\\overset{?}{=}-4\\left(2 e^{- 4 x}\\right) \\\\ - 8 e^{- 4 x} &=- 8 e^{- 4 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=2 e^{- 4 x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "fe53f718-81c9-57aa-bc08-4cf2e12fab5d",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
